# Bloodline Performance Rules

## Core Performance Philosophy

The platform must feel:

- fast
- responsive
- smooth
- stable
- lightweight

Premium experience must never become:
```txt
heavy or sluggish
```

Performance is part of brand perception.

---

## Rendering Philosophy

The platform is:

```txt
Server-first
SEO-first
Performance-first
```

Prefer:
- Server Components
- static rendering
- ISR
- minimal hydration

Avoid:
- unnecessary client rendering
- hydration-heavy pages
- runtime-heavy interactions

---

## Hydration Rules

Hydration should remain:
- minimal
- isolated
- intentional

Client Components should exist only where interaction is required.

Avoid:
- hydrating entire pages
- wrapping layouts with unnecessary providers
- client-side rendering creep

---

## JavaScript Rules

Ship as little JavaScript as possible.

Avoid:
- oversized client bundles
- unnecessary dependencies
- browser-heavy rendering
- giant client-side state systems

The site should remain:
```txt
content-first
```

---

## Dependency Rules

Before adding any package:
- validate necessity
- validate maintenance quality
- validate bundle impact

Avoid:
- dependency bloat
- overlapping libraries
- experimental heavy packages

---

## Image Rules

Always use:
```tsx
next/image
```

Required:
- responsive sizing
- lazy loading
- optimized formats
- proper sizing strategy

Avoid:
- raw `<img>` tags
- oversized source images
- loading full-resolution media unnecessarily

---

## Gallery Performance Rules

Gallery systems should:
- lazy load images
- paginate or virtualize when needed
- avoid loading huge grids immediately

Avoid:
- rendering massive image datasets at once
- giant client-side gallery payloads

The gallery should feel:
```txt
smooth and responsive
```

---

## Video Rules

Videos should:
- remain optimized
- avoid blocking rendering
- lazy load where possible

Avoid:
- autoplay overload
- giant uncompressed hero videos
- multiple simultaneous background videos

Video should support atmosphere,
not dominate performance budget.

---

## Motion Performance Rules

Motion should prioritize:
- transform animations
- opacity transitions
- GPU-safe rendering

Avoid:
- layout-thrashing animation
- CPU-heavy motion systems
- oversized animation orchestration

Motion must remain:
```txt
performance-safe
```

---

## Scroll Performance Rules

Avoid:
- heavy scroll listeners
- giant parallax systems
- scroll hijacking
- expensive continuous calculations

Scroll interactions should remain:
- lightweight
- responsive
- stable

---

## Client Component Rules

Client Components should remain:
- small
- isolated
- interaction-focused

Avoid:
- client-rendering SEO content
- giant interactive wrappers
- unnecessary browser state

---

## Provider Rules

Avoid excessive global providers.

Only use providers when:
- truly required
- architecture-critical

Avoid:
- provider stacking
- unnecessary global client wrappers

---

## Font Performance Rules

Use:
```txt
next/font
```

Required:
- optimized loading
- minimal layout shift

Avoid:
- blocking external font loading
- excessive font families
- oversized font variants

---

## Data Fetching Rules

Prefer:
- server-side data preparation
- ISR
- caching
- normalized datasets

Avoid:
- repeated client fetching
- unnecessary runtime requests
- duplicate fetch logic

---

## Airtable Performance Rules

Never fetch Airtable directly from UI components.

Use:
- normalization layer
- cached structures
- server-side preparation

Avoid:
- client-side Airtable access
- runtime-heavy Airtable rendering

---

## Review System Rules

Reviews should:
- render from cached JSON
- support ISR
- avoid runtime scraping

Avoid:
- live-fetching reviews on page load

---

## MDX Performance Rules

MDX should:
- compile efficiently
- remain lightweight
- avoid giant interactive embeds

Avoid:
- unnecessary heavy MDX runtime systems
- oversized dynamic MDX rendering

---

## Bundle Rules

Continuously minimize:
- bundle size
- hydration cost
- client-side logic

Avoid:
- importing large libraries globally
- oversized animation packages
- dead dependencies

---

## Route Performance Rules

SEO-critical routes should:
- render quickly
- avoid client dependency
- avoid excessive runtime logic

Priority routes:
- homepage
- styles
- services
- blog articles

---

## Lazy Loading Rules

Lazy load:
- below-the-fold media
- heavy galleries
- non-critical components
- optional interactions

Avoid:
- lazy-loading critical above-the-fold content

---

## Core Web Vitals Rules

Prioritize:
- low LCP
- low CLS
- low TBT
- fast interaction readiness

Avoid:
- layout shift
- unstable media sizing
- delayed content rendering

---

## Mobile Performance Rules

Mobile performance takes priority.

Optimize for:
- weak networks
- mid-range devices
- touch responsiveness

Avoid:
- desktop-only optimization thinking

---

## Accessibility Performance Rules

Performance optimizations must never:
- reduce accessibility
- hide semantic content
- break keyboard interaction

Maintain:
- usability
- readability
- stability

---

## Caching Rules

Prefer:
- ISR
- static rendering
- predictable cache strategy

Avoid:
- unnecessary no-store rendering
- unstable cache invalidation

---

## SEO Performance Rules

Performance supports SEO.

Maintain:
- server-rendered content
- fast route delivery
- crawlable structure
- lightweight rendering

Avoid:
- JS-heavy SEO pages
- client-only metadata systems

---

## Monitoring Rules

Continuously monitor:
- Lighthouse
- bundle size
- hydration cost
- route performance
- Core Web Vitals

Performance regressions should be treated seriously.

---

## AI Performance Safety Rules

AI-generated implementation must:
- minimize hydration
- minimize dependencies
- preserve ISR
- preserve server rendering
- avoid unnecessary client logic

Avoid:
- shipping complexity unnecessarily
- introducing heavy libraries casually
- bloating interaction systems

---

## Forbidden Patterns

Never introduce:

- giant client-side state systems
- oversized bundles
- autoplay chaos
- giant parallax systems
- direct Airtable fetching in components
- raw unoptimized media
- unnecessary providers
- hydration-heavy SEO pages
- layout-thrashing animation

---

## Final Principle

Every performance decision should reinforce:

- speed
- responsiveness
- stability
- SEO quality
- mobile experience
- premium perception

If an implementation weakens performance significantly, do not implement it.